(Darren – interview5_iteration 3)

Interviewer: So, what was your first impression when you open this platform?
Interviewee: When I first opened it, my impression was that it was clean and well organized. The home page clearly showed the club posts with a photo, category label, and the member counts. There was a search bar at the top, and below that are category buttons like sports, faculty, culture and others. This layout made it easy to explore without being overwhelmed by too much text at once. The design looked modern and very similar to common social apps. So I felt I could start using it almost immediately.

Interviewer: OK, how easy was it to find a club that matches your interest?
Interviewee: It was quite easy to find the clubs that match my interest. For example, from the recommended club section, I could see the EQBlessing students association clearly labeled under culture with 315 members. The category filters at the top of the screen including Sports, faculty, culture, others help me narrow down what I was looking for. If I'm interested in culture activities, I can tap on culture and see all culture related clubs together. This makes the search process much faster than scrolling through the long uncategorized list.

Interviewer: OK, understand. And did the search function and club categories help you discover relevant clubs?
Interviewee: Yes, they did. The search bar at the top is very useful. If I already know the specific club name, I can search directly. Even without a specific club in mind, the category buttons allow me to explore based on my general interest. For instance, I could instantly see that there is a Malaysia culture club, a Korean culture club, and a computing society. The recommended club selections also shows a mix of popular clubs, which helps me to discover groups I might not have thought about, like the photography club and game club.

Interviewer: Were there any part of the interface that you found confusing or difficult to use?
Interviewee: Most of the interface is straightforward, but I noticed one slightly confusing point. The navigation bar at the bottom has only three tabs: Home, Clubs and Profile. The club page lists all clubs, while home shows recommended clubs. At first I was not fully sure what's the difference between Home and Clubs. After a few clicks I figured it out: Home is a personalized dashboard while Clubs is a full directory. This is not a big problem, but completely new users may need a short explanation.

Interviewer: Did the club detail page provide enough information for you to decide whether to join a club?
Interviewee: The club details page provided useful basic information. This included an introduction section describing the club's purpose, a list of weekly activities and meeting information. However, for me to decide if I want to join, I would also like to see upcoming event dates or a simple calendar. Besides, adding photos or videos of past events would make the club feel more vivid and help me understand its atmosphere. While the current information is good, visual content would make it even better.

Interviewer: So you need some photos to help you understand what this club is. OK, sure. How easy was it to navigate between different pages and features on the platform?
Interviewee: Transition between pages was very easy. The bottom navigation bar stays the same on every screen with clear labels: Home, Clubs, Profile. Tapping on Clubs takes me to the full list, and tapping on a club card brings me to its detail page. From the detail page, I can easily go back using the back arrow on the top left, or jump to another section using the bottom tabs. The flow feels logical and consistent with no unexpected jumps. This makes the platform responsive and user friendly.

Interviewer: What information or features do you think are missing from the platform?
Interviewee: I hope to add a calendar and upcoming events directly on the club detail page. A photo or video gallery to display past activities and club atmosphere is also needed. Besides, a join button on the detail page allows users to sign up directly. In addition, reviews or comments from current and former members can offer honest references for users to make decisions.

Interviewer: OK. And how useful do you think this platform would be for new UQ students looking to get involved on campus?
Interviewee: I think this platform would be very useful, especially for new students. When you first arrive at university, you often do not know which clubs exist, where to find them or what they actually do. This platform presents all clubs in a simple, visual and searchable way. Category filters allow new students to explore based on their personal interests, and the club details pages give a quick overview of cooperations and activities. Compared to asking friends or searching on social media, this is much more efficient for newcomers.

Interviewer: Compared to other ways to finding clubs, for example, UQ official websites, social media and asking friends, what do you think of this platform's performance?
Interviewee: Compared to traditional club lists on UQ official websites, this platform is much more lively and interactive. Official web pages often contain long texts and tables with no images, which feel outdated. Social media can be good for viewing events, but information is scattered across different accounts and takes time to find. Asking friends is helpful but limited by their social circle. This platform combines structured data like official directories and card-based design similar to social media. It makes club discovery faster and more enjoyable.

Interviewer: OK, sure. If this platform were officially available, how likely would you be to use it in the future and why?
Interviewee: I would be very likely to use this platform in the future. Firstly, it saves my time. It has clear layout and simple navigation, and reduces the trouble of searching among numerous clubs. Also, it helps me discover some clubs I never knew before.
